After some more investigation this is currently only a problem if you try to then send the payload as a body.

Where the problem actually is that k6 tries to seriliaze it but it uses fmt.Sprintf on a cyclic `map[string]interface{}` which is not ... [supported](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/8241)

https://github.com/grafana/k6/blob/6aa49bc8a2fcbc2a2b3c652a52d2a88a9dc16521/js/modules/k6/http/request.go#L261C2-L263

https://github.com/grafana/k6/blob/6aa49bc8a2fcbc2a2b3c652a52d2a88a9dc16521/js/modules/k6/http/request.go#L177-L180

Unfortunately I am not certain how used this functionality is ... and whether fixing it is even a possibility. And has been around for more then 5 years at this point.

But this also means that this is likely happening in a lot more places then we think as I am pretty sure we have a lot of places where we print arguments from user scripts ... usually with no checks.

Given that nobody else has hit it .. it seems unlikely to be a big enough issue.

A simple reproduction is 
```javascript
var http = require("k6/http");
exports.default =  function() {
    var s ={ }
    s.inner = s;
    var res = http.post("https://httpbin.k6.io", s);
}
``` 


As a proposed solution - we will likely need to change this to actually work directly with the goja.Value in some way, which looking at the code will not be ... easy.
